{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ROUND 9 = PASS (0 blocking, 9 advisory). Attestation SIGNED and CERTIFIED at 2b5bb00cdf5f559ea644e3c853cd97cbc2220bc8; ticket CLAIMED. Nine rounds total.\n\nDECISION ON THE QUEUED REFINEMENT — carried into IMPLEMENTATION rather than into the plan, deliberately. The refinement (read REBAR_LLM_MODEL ONCE in `parse_class_slots` and pass the default down, instead of once per class inside `_parse_slot`, so one `load_class_slots()` call emits ONE warning rather than three) is an implementation choice that the certified plan PERMITS rather than contradicts: Scope requires the fan-out and its literal env read to live in `model_classes.py`, and `parse_class_slots` is in `model_classes.py`. Editing the plan to mandate it would be a MATERIAL edit, invalidating a PASS that took nine rounds, to specify something no AC constrains and that I am about to implement correctly anyway.\n\nSo it is recorded HERE as the binding implementation decision, and it will be enforced by a TEST rather than by plan text: one `load_class_slots()` call with the variable set emits EXACTLY ONE warning. That is a per-CALL count, NOT once-per-process — a second call must warn again, since the once-per-process requirement was withdrawn as unmechanised. If a future reader wonders why the plan is silent on warning multiplicity, this is why."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785591314068332001,"uuid":"ca9f5e2a-c86a-45df-83ac-a57f0df2d889"}